"hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:"
2845,"Narrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Hisham. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, narrated to us Hisham. And narrated to us Muhammad ibnul-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, narrated to us 'Abdur-Rahim — meaning ibn Sulayman. And narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki'. And narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, informed us Muhammad ibn Dinar — all from Hisham ibn 'Urwah. This is the narration of 'Abdul-Jabbar, and it is the most excellent in arrangement.

Hisham ibn 'Urwah said: I heard my father (RA) say: I heard Usamah (RA) — and he was beside me, and he was the riding companion of the Prophet (ﷺ) from 'Arafah — asking: ""How did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ride when he set out from 'Arafah?""

He (Usamah, RA) said: ""He (ﷺ) would ride at a moderate pace (al-'anaq). When he found an open space, he would urge the camel to a faster pace (nasṣ).""

Sufyan (RA) said: ""Nasṣ is faster than 'anaq.""
Abu Bakr (RA) said: In his narration, it is inserted (mudraj), and ""nasṣ"" is higher in degree than ""‘anaq"". In the narration of Waki', it is also inserted in the hadith, meaning above ""‘anaq"".

**CHAPTER:**"
